<!--
  Unlike the other files in this repo, this one was not produced by running
  lirc2xml/DecodeIR over a captured lircd.conf. It was hand-derived from the
  timing/code table in:

    sony/RMT-B118P.lirc.conf, contributed by Jose Riha <jose1711@gmail.com>
    to https://github.com/jose1711/lirc_remotes (based on RMT-B104P),
    documented there for the Sony BDP-S185 Blu-Ray Disc Player.

  RMT-B118P/RMT-B118A/RMT-B119A/RMT-B117A/RMT-B109C are cross-listed by
  Sony/retailers as the same functional remote (same layout, same protocol
  family) across the BDP-BX18/BX39/BX59/BX110/BX310/BX510, BDP-S185/S390/
  S590/S1100/S3100/S5100/S780 generation - which includes the BDP-BX510.

  The Pronto Hex <ccf> values were computed from the conf's header/one/zero
  timings and 12-bit (+8-bit post_data) codes using this repo's established
  Sony SIRC layout: word bits sent MSB..LSB, then post_data bits MSB..LSB,
  OBC = bitreverse7(word>>5), device = bitreverse5(word&0x1F), subdevice =
  bitreverse8(post_data). This was validated two ways before being trusted:
  (1) re-deriving the already-present sony/RMT-B104P.xml entries (identical
  protocol family) byte-for-byte from the same formulas - every bit-pulse
  pair matched exactly, only the synthesized trailing gap-filler word (a
  few cycles, <150us out of a ~13ms pad) differing, which does not affect
  playback; (2) cross-checking every derived OBC against hifi-remote.com's
  Sony Blu-ray device-code table (device 26, subdevice group 226/234/242 -
  http://www.hifi-remote.com/sony/Sony_bluray.htm), which documents this as
  the modern/current BD command set (covers Netflix/SEN/3D/Bluetooth) - all
  37 buttons matched their documented command exactly (21=Power, 24=Stop,
  26=Play, 99=Subtitle, 76=SEN, etc), with zero mismatches.

  Each <ccf> bakes in 3 back-to-back copies of the frame (not just 1, as
  elsewhere in this repo) because real Sony remotes retransmit every code a
  minimum of three times per press - a lone single frame is commonly
  rejected as noise by Sony SIRC receivers (see
  righto.com/2010/03/understanding-sony-ir-remote-codes-lirc.html). Baking
  the repeats into the "once" section means a single tap already satisfies
  this even in apps that don't implement Pronto's separate hold/repeat
  section. decoding hex0-3 (DecodeIR's alternate-protocol guesses) could
  not be reproduced without running DecodeIR itself and are left -1.
-->
